# Nothing catches a missing @gc_slots

**DONE.** `census_gc_slots` reports it on every build. Opened and
closed the same day as `tasks/093`, which created the omission it
guards.

## The shape of the miss

A bound class that holds an `nb::object` and does NOT carry
`@gc_slots(...)` leaks itself the moment that object closes over it.
Silent: the suite passes, and nanobind reports it at interpreter
shutdown, after the last test.

It is this repo's named failure mode with a new face. NOT an emitter
skipping what it does not recognise - the emitter wrote exactly what
the declaration said. The declaration omitted something no rule
required it to say, so the rule is what was missing.

## What was written

`census_gc_slots(have)`, beside `census_cpp` and `census_markers`,
printed on every build:

    gc slots: eval.hpp stores a Python object, and EvalState says
    how to traverse it

PER FILE, and that is a limit rather than a shortcut. The `Evaluator`
this exists for does not hold the callables itself - `EvalCore` does,
and nothing binds `EvalCore` - so a per-class check would have to
follow C++ members through a type no declaration mentions. "Does any
declaration over this file say it" is the question a text scan can
answer honestly.

**It would have caught the original.** `eval.hpp` held the callable
and no declaration anywhere carried the marker.

## The member test, and why it is not a parse

A C++ parser here would be a second compiler. The test is: the line
carries `nb::object`, `nb::callable` or `nb::handle`, ends in `;`,
and holds NO parenthesis.

That last clause is what does the work, and it was measured on the
real file. The naive version matched two lines:

    std::vector<nb::object> primops_;          the member
    nb::object fn) const;                      a wrapped parameter

The second is the tail of `register_primop`'s declaration. Excluding
any line with a parenthesis drops it and keeps the member.

What it misses is stated in the docstring rather than hidden: a
member reached through a typedef, a template parameter, or a
`std::function` wrapping a callable.

## Printed, not raised

`census_markers`'s reason, and it applies harder here. A helper
storing a Python object may be safe for a reason only a person knows,
and failing the build would get the marker added blindly rather than
thought about.

## Proved by breaking it

Removing `@gc_slots` from the declaration:

    markers: 25 declared, 23 used
      @gc_slots: no declaration carries it - the emitter branches
      behind it have never run
      eval.hpp: stores a Python object and NO declaration over it
      carries @gc_slots - such a class leaks itself whenever that
      object closes over it (tasks/093)
        std::vector<nb::object> primops_;

TWO reports for one omission, and that is worth keeping rather than
deduplicating. `census_markers` notices the marker is unused;
this notices the file that needs it. Either alone could be read as
noise; together they name the fix.

Opened and closed 2026-09-05, from `tasks/093`.
